 ## Overview
-This method projects a 3D Gaussian primitive, defined by a mean and a covariance matrix in world space, into a 2D elliptical footprint (splat) on the image plane for rasterization [S1, S3, unofficial]. It solves the problem of rendering millions of anisotropic 3D Gaussians in real-time by avoiding ray marching; instead, it approximates the non-linear perspective projection with a local affine transformation, preserving the Gaussian form in 2D [S3, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from [Optimal Gaussian Splatting Projection](3311-optimal-gaussian-splatting-projection.md) by using a fixed image plane rather than a per-Gaussian tangent plane, and from [2D Gaussian Covariance Stabilization and Rescaling](3467-2d-gaussian-covariance-stabilization-and-rescaling.md) by adding a constant epsilon to the diagonal for positive definiteness rather than rescaling the determinant [S2, unofficial].
+2D Gaussian Projection with Covariance Stabilization is the core rasterization primitive of 3D Gaussian Splatting that maps an anisotropic 3D Gaussian volume primitive into a 2D elliptical footprint (splat) on the image plane [S3, unofficial]. It solves the problem of rendering millions of 3D probability distributions efficiently by approximating the non-linear perspective projection with a local affine transformation, thereby preserving the Gaussian form in screen space [S3, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Optimal Gaussian Splatting Projection](3311-optimal-gaussian-splatting-projection.md) by using a fixed image-plane projection rather than per-Gaussian tangent planes, and from [Render Means](2070-render-means.md) by explicitly computing the screen-space covariance matrix to handle shape and spread [S1, unofficial]. The method stabilizes the projection by adding a small epsilon to the diagonal of the 2D covariance matrix to guarantee positive definiteness and prevent aliasing [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs:**
-* $M$ 3D Gaussians, each defined by:
-    * Mean $\mu_w \in \mathbb{R}^3$ (world coordinates) [S8, unofficial].
-    * Covariance $\Sigma_w \in \mathbb{R}^{3 \times 3}$ (symmetric positive semi-definite), often stored as a rotation quaternion $q$ and scale vector $s$ where $\Sigma_w = R S S^\top R^\top$ [S1, S7, unofficial].
-    * Opacity $\alpha \in [0, 1]$ [S7, unofficial].
-    * Color coefficients (e.g., Spherical Harmonics) [S7, unofficial].
-* Camera parameters: View matrix $V$, Projection matrix $P$, image width $W$, height $H$, focal lengths $f_x, f_y$, principal point $c_x, c_y$ [S1, S7, unofficial].
+*   $M$ 3D Gaussians, each defined by:
+    *   Mean position in world space $\mu_w \in \mathbb{R}^3$.
+    *   Rotation quaternion $q \in \mathbb{R}^4$ (normalized).
+    *   Scale vector $s \in \mathbb{R}^3$ (log-scale parameters).
+    *   Opacity $\alpha \in [0, 1]$.
